(1333171, 168628, 33267, '<td valign="top">Thanks for those who liked the picture.  Yes it''s the moon taken with my rebel true a Meade 8in telescope... during the week of the challenge!<br/> <br/>Off course many commented the picture as being too soft and not being well focused, but I just gotta say that the moon is about 380,000ft from the earth so you have to deal with: <br/>1. light polution, 2. humidity, 3. soft clouds, 4. very ow light (so high ISO) 5. the movement of the earth vs the moon with a very high magnification.  So astrophotography is not as easy as shooting an object on the earth and it''s almost impossible to get a good crisp image.  I was very happy since it was my fisrt attempt and it''s not so bad as my score tell moreover when I compare it to other moon pictures I saw on Internet.  Of course you''ll have more cripier image taken by hubble or any other devide that is not under the atmosphere and that is built by the NASA!<br/> <br/>Thanks again for those who liked it for what it really is: scientific passion to see the moon as close as possible and not expacting to have the picture as crisp as if it was Neil Armstrong who took it! :)</td>', 'Thanks for those who liked the picture.  Yes it''s the moon taken with my rebel true a Meade 8in telescope... during the week of the challenge! Off course many commented the picture as being too soft and not being well focused, but I just gotta say that the moon is about 380,000ft from the earth so you have to deal with: 1. light polution, 2. humidity, 3. soft clouds, 4. very ow light (so high ISO) 5. the movement of the earth vs the moon with a very high magnification.  So astrophotography is not as easy as shooting an object on the earth and it''s almost impossible to get a good crisp image.  I was very happy since it was my fisrt attempt and it''s not so bad as my score tell moreover when I compare it to other moon pictures I saw on Internet.  Of course you''ll have more cripier image taken by hubble or any other devide that is not under the atmosphere and that is built by the NASA! Thanks again for those who liked it for what it really is: scientific passion to see the moon as close as possible and not expacting to have the picture as crisp as if it was Neil Armstrong who took it! :)', '2005-04-27 13:50:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(1333288, 168621, 5286, '<td valign="top">Congrats on the blue!! I was about to leave a comment on your People picture last night saying it was only a matter of time before you ribbon... and these results came out right then :) <br/>Lovely shot!!  <br/></td>', 'Congrats on the blue!! I was about to leave a comment on your People picture last night saying it was only a matter of time before you ribbon... and these results came out right then :) Lovely shot!!  ', '2005-04-27 14:05:37.000000', NULL, 0),
